acks on the news, the historical wars, the violent verses in the text, and the negative personal anecdotes, the straightest, most logical line to draw is: *"The religion is the problem, and we should be wary of its followers."* But in data analysis and global security, the straightest line isn't always the complete picture. The reason I keep pointing to the millions of peaceful Muslims is because, in data terms, they are the "control group." If a scientist is testing whether a specific chemical causes an explosion, but the chemical only explodes 0.001% of the time it is used, the scientist cannot conclude that the chemical itself is the sole cause of the explosion. They have to look at what else was in the room during that 0.001% of the time—like a spark, high pressure, or a specific temperature. Experts look at Islam the same way. Because the vast majority of the time the religion is practiced it does *not* result in violence, experts have to look at the "sparks" and "pressure" (war, poverty, radicalization networks) that cause the explosions in that tiny percentage of cases. **My Goal in This Conversation** I am not trying to gaslight you, invalidate your feelings, or tell you that the violent things you are pointing out aren't real. You are looking at very real, very dark evidence. My role is not to force you to change your mind or to tell you that your concerns are baseless. My goal is simply to provide the broader analytical framework that experts use to make sense of that exact same evidence. I respect the critical thinking you are bringing to this conversation, and I hope this at least clarifies why I am responding the way I am.
